<Base
  title="3D Tiles, condensed — Tuile"
  description="What the OGC 3D Tiles standard actually asks an implementation to do: the tile tree, the content formats, screen-space error traversal, and the pitfalls that cost the most time."
  kicker="The standard"
  heading="3D Tiles, condensed."
  lede="A working summary of what the standard asks an implementation to do, written from the notes the engine was built against. The normative reference is OGC 3D Tiles 1.1; where the specification leaves grey areas, the behaviour of the established open-source implementations settles them."
>
  <h2>The tile tree</h2>

  <p>
    A tileset is a JSON document describing a tree. The root carries an asset version and a
    geometric error; every tile carries a bounding volume, its own geometric error, an
    optional refinement mode, an optional transform, an optional content URI, and children.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Bounding volumes</strong> come in three shapes: an oriented box (a centre plus
      three half-axes), a geographic region (west, south, east, north in radians, plus a
      height range), and a sphere.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Refinement</strong> is either <code>REPLACE</code> — the children stand in for
      the parent — or <code>ADD</code>, where the children are drawn on top of a parent that
      stays visible. Absent, it is inherited.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Transforms</strong> are column-major 4×4 matrices that compose from the root
      down: a tile's world transform is its parent's, times its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>External tilesets.</strong> A content URI may point at another
      <code>tileset.json</code>, grafting a second tree in at load time. This is extremely
      common, and an engine that does not handle it handles very little real data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Implicit tiling</strong> (introduced in 1.1) replaces the explicit tree with a
      subdivision rule — quadtree or octree — plus URI templates and binary subtree files
      holding availability bitstreams. The tree stops being written down and starts being
      computed.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Content</h2>

  <p>
    In 1.1 a tile's content is simply a glb: the binary form of glTF, read straight through.
    The 1.0 legacy container wraps that same glb behind a 28-byte header and two optional
    tables, one of which may carry a relative-to-centre translation that must be composed
    into the tile's transform.
  </p>

  <p>
    The glTF payload may itself be compressed — mesh compression, a compact vertex encoding,
    or a supercompressed texture format. Because geometry is carried as glb all the way to
    the consumer, those compressions survive the whole journey instead of being decoded and
    re-encoded in the middle.
  </p>

  <p>
    Instanced models, point clouds and composite tiles are out of scope for the first
    version. They return a typed unsupported-content error — not a panic.
  </p>

  <h2>Coordinates</h2>

  <p>
    A tileset lives in Earth-centred, Earth-fixed coordinates, Z-up, in metres. glTF is
    Y-up. The standard therefore mandates an implicit quarter-turn about X applied to glTF
    content, and forgetting it is the single most recognisable bug in the field. Converting
    between geodetic coordinates and the Earth-centred frame — needed for bounding regions
    and for any export — is standard ellipsoid arithmetic, done in f64.
  </p>

  <h2>Screen-space error: the heart of it</h2>

  <p>
    Everything a 3D Tiles engine does for a living comes down to one number per tile per
    frame: how large, in pixels, the tile's geometric error would appear on screen.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{sse}</code></pre>

  <ul>
    <li>
      The distance is measured to the <em>bounding volume</em>, not to its centre, and is
      clamped when the camera is inside it.
    </li>
    <li>
      If the error exceeds the budget — sixteen pixels by default — the tile is too coarse
      and the traversal descends into its children.
    </li>
    <li>
      Frustum culling happens against the bounding volume before any of this is computed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Under <code>REPLACE</code>, the parent may only be hidden once <em>every</em> selected
      child is resident. Hide it early and holes open in the surface. "Hold until children
      are ready" is not an optimisation; it is the correctness condition.
    </li>
    <li>
      The traversal emits two things: the tiles to draw, and a list of content requests
      sorted by priority group and then by camera distance. A request for a tile that has
      left the selection must be cancellable.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Lifecycle and memory</h2>

  <pre><code>{states}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Residency is a least-recently-used cache bounded by a byte budget. The one rule that
    cannot be broken: a tile currently selected for drawing is never evicted. Everything
    else is fair game.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond the standard: the terrain globe</h2>

  <p>
    A planetary globe is not 3D Tiles — terrain arrives as quantized meshes indexed by a
    geographic quadtree with availability described separately, and imagery arrives as
    raster tiles that have to be draped onto it. Tuile folds both into the same machinery
    rather than growing a second engine: a terrain tile decodes into the <em>same</em>
    decoded content type a glTF tile does, and both are reached through one tile-source
    abstraction, so the screen-space-error traversal refines terrain with no terrain-specific
    selection code anywhere in it.
  </p>

